BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sight-geist-rubin-foundation-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260821T065440Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sight/Geist Season 7: Film & Performance Open Call for Em
 erging NYC Artists
DESCRIPTION:Seventh season of Sight/Geist\, a series from the Shelley & Don
 ald Rubin Foundation supporting emerging NYC-based film and performance ar
 tists. The Foundation provides curatorial\, administrative and promotional
  support\, plus documentation for performances and discussions\; selected 
 artists also engage in a Q&A on their broader practice. TWO CATEGORIES: (1
 ) single-channel screening - experimental/non-commercial cinema\, video ar
 t or performance documentation as a single-channel non-looped projection w
 ith 2.1 PA\; H.264 or ProRes\; under 80 minutes (priority to <20 min)\; (2
 ) performance and expanded forms - 20-50 minutes\; may foreground duration
 \, movement\, audiovisual media\, voice\, site specificity\, audience part
 icipation\; conceptual and political proposals encouraged. ELIGIBILITY: pr
 imary applicant must be at least 18 and maintain their primary residence i
 n New York City (or spend most of their artistic\, professional and social
  life in NYC)\; self-identify as an 'emerging' artist (early-career\, self
 -trained\, newly graduated\, or currently enrolled in undergraduate/gradua
 te programs)\; one submission per artist/duo/collective. Submissions open 
 1 June through 11 pm ET\, 5 July 2026\; applicants notified by late August
  2026. Contact: info@the8thfloor.org.\n\nOrganisation: Shelley & Donald Ru
 bin Foundation (The 8th Floor)\nLocation: The 8th Floor\, near Union Squar
 e\, New York City\, United States\nAward: Fixed artist fees: $300 for a gr
 oup screening\; $500 for a solo screening\; $750 for a solo performance\, 
 with an additional $250 for reimbursable production costs (collaborators\,
  materials\, transportation\, rehearsal\, specific AV/lighting beyond the 
 gallery inventory). For duos and collectives the fees are shared. The prim
 ary applicant must be able to submit an invoice and US tax form to receive
  payment. No application fee.\nDuration: 2026/27 programming season at The
  8th Floor\n\nMore: https://www.sdrubin.org/artists?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdrubin.org/artists?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The 8th Floor\, near Union Square\, New York City\, United States
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adrianna-newell-fellowship-artsrec-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260821T065440Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Adrianna Newell Fellowship at Arts&Rec 2026
DESCRIPTION:Inaugural Adrianna Newell Fellowship\, a live/work studio resid
 ency in Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills) for October 2026\, with possible
  extension into November. Named for board member Adrianna Newell\, it invi
 tes queer lens-based artists working in photography and moving image with 
 an active\, research-driven or conceptually rich practice\, especially wor
 k engaging queer perspectives on land\, rurality and regional space\, or q
 ueer identity\, memory\, intimacy\, community and embodiment. Open to arti
 sts from everywhere. Fellows receive a 1000 sq ft studio with attached apa
 rtment\, a $1\,500 combined materials stipend and per diem\, and introduct
 ions to the local creative community\; an open-studio presentation\, artis
 t talk or public exhibition at the end is required. Travel\, especially in
 ternational\, is not funded (residents are encouraged to seek travel suppo
 rt elsewhere). Apply by emailing a single PDF (one-page description of you
 r work and planned project\, 10 to 20 work samples with short descriptions
 \, and a current CV) to programs@artsrec.org. Deadline: 31 July 2026\; not
 ices by end of August.\n\nOrganisation: Arts&Rec Inc\nLocation: Stamford\,
  NY (Western Catskills)\, USA\nAward: Live/work residency: use of a 1000 s
 q ft studio and attached apartment\, plus a $1\,500 total materials stipen
 d and per diem. Travel (especially international) is not funded. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: October 2026 (four weeks to two months\, based on pr
 oject needs\; possible extension into November)\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.
 org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-applications%3A-the-adr
 ianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&id=013e03ad-6edf-47f
 b-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-app
 lications%3A-the-adrianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&
 id=013e03ad-6edf-47fb-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills)\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pandora-short-film-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260821T065440Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pandora Short Film Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Pandora Short Film Grant is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit that awar
 ds an annual $12\,000 grant to an early-career US filmmaker for the produc
 tion of a narrative short film. Projects at script stage or already filmed
  and seeking finishing funds are both eligible. The winner also receives a
  Panavision camera package (about $15\,000)\, an optional $1\,000 Kodak Fi
 lm Grant\, mentorship from an award-winning filmmaker (2026 mentors includ
 e Alma Har'el\, Lila Neugebauer\, Lauren Wolkstein\, Sarah Elizabeth Mintz
  and Chris Merola)\, and fiscal sponsorship\; three finalists receive ment
 orship\, fiscal sponsorship\, Panavision New Filmmaker Program considerati
 on and discounted production insurance. ELIGIBILITY: open to all early-car
 eer US filmmakers\; applicants are asked NOT to apply if they have directe
 d a feature film with a budget over $250K or directed or produced televisi
 on. Selection is blind\, based on script and pitch materials\, weighting a
 rtistic vision over industry resume. TIMELINE: submissions open 30 May 202
 6\, close 1 October 2026\, notifications 15 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation
 : Pandora Short Film Grant\nLocation: United States (early-career US filmm
 akers)\nAward: $12\,000 production grant to the winner\, plus a Panavision
  camera package (worth about $15\,000)\, an optional $1\,000 Kodak Film Gr
 ant toward film stock and lab expenses\, mentorship from an award-winning 
 filmmaker\, and fiscal sponsorship. Three finalists receive mentorship\, f
 iscal sponsorship\, consideration for the Panavision New Filmmaker Program
 \, and $500 off Athos production insurance. ENTRY FEE: tiered\, $30 regula
 r ($27 Gold members) and $40 late ($36)\; Pandora states fee waivers are a
 vailable on request.\nDuration: Production grant for a narrative short fil
 m (script stage or finishing funds)\n\nMore: https://pandora.org/?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pandora.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:United States (early-career US filmmakers)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:legalization-project-immigrant-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260821T065440Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Legalization Project: Immigrant Content Creators Prog
 ram 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:The Legalization Project is running a nine-month programme for 
 experienced content creators who want to make engaging\, accessible work a
 bout U.S. immigration law\, policy and the issues affecting immigrant comm
 unities. Rather than commissioning one-off pieces\, it pairs a USD 10\,000
  stipend with actual legal grounding: training in U.S. immigration law and
  policy\, ongoing updates from immigration attorneys and advocates\, and c
 ontinuous feedback on the content you make. This suits creators already wo
 rking in video\, social or short-form documentary who want to report accur
 ately on a fast-moving legal area rather than guess at it. ELIGIBILITY: ex
 perienced content creators who are immigrants. The programme is explicitly
  open to immigrants of ALL statuses. APPLICATION: preliminary applications
  are due 17 August 2026\, through the programme page at legalizationprojec
 t.notion.site/icc-program (also circulated as bit.ly/icc-program). Being a
  preliminary application\, expect a second stage after this date.\n\nOrgan
 isation: The Legalization Project\nLocation: United States focus (content 
 covers U.S. immigration law and policy). Programme delivery format not spe
 cified in the call.\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend\, plus training in U.S. im
 migration law and policy\, regular updates from immigration attorneys and 
 advocates\, and ongoing feedback and support on your content. No applicati
 on fee.\nDuration: Nine months\n\nMore: https://legalizationproject.notion
 .site/icc-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://legalizationproject.notion.site/icc-program?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States focus (content covers U.S. immigration law and polic
 y). Programme delivery format not specified in the call.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bavc-mediamaker-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260821T065440Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship is a ten-month intensive for doc
 umentary filmmakers using bold cinematic language and innovative impact st
 rategies to take on critical issues of the moment. Each fellow receives US
 D 10\,000 in unrestricted funding paid out across the programme\, plus tra
 vel per diems\, and joins a cohort programme of workshops\, feedback sessi
 ons\, mentorship and industry access rather than a one-off cash award. ELI
 GIBILITY: directors of a first or second feature-length documentary\, base
 d primarily in the United States. The project must be non-commercial and f
 eature-length (shorts\, series and podcasts are excluded)\, primarily docu
 mentary or non-fiction including hybrid forms\, and demonstrate potential 
 for social impact. Only one applicant per filmmaking team may apply\, and 
 full-time students are not eligible. PROJECT STAGE: eligible films must ha
 ve at least begun production and may have started post-production\, but sh
 ould not reach a fine cut before the programme year ends\, since the point
  is to workshop the film in progress. APPLICATION: the first step is an In
 tent to Apply\, submitted through the Airtable form linked from the progra
 mme page\, due 30 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: BAVC Media (Bay Ar
 ea Video Coalition)\nLocation: United States\; applicants must primarily r
 eside in the U.S. and not expect to live abroad for a major part of the ye
 ar. Mostly remote\, with two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel
  covered).\nAward: USD 10\,000 distributed to each Fellow over the course 
 of the fellowship\, plus travel per diems. Non-cash support includes fligh
 ts and accommodation for two San Francisco convenings\, festival and confe
 rence access with travel covered\, professional mentorship and strategy se
 ssions\, five hours of one-to-one mentorship through MediaMaker Connect\, 
 and ongoing workshops. Bay Area fellows also get access to BAVC's co-worki
 ng space and facilities. No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-month fellowsh
 ip\n\nMore: https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
